import { Node, html.div, html.h2, html.h3, html.p, html.strong } from "web";

export Node Manual() {
  return <div class="kc-panel kc-manual">
    <h2>Emergency field manual</h2>
    <p>Expert copy · R/V Nereid. Have the operator read the wet sensor, fault code, depth, floodwater and battery aloud.</p>
    <h3>01 / Isolate the leak</h3>
    <p>Close the bulkhead listed for the wet sensor. Closing a different bulkhead blocks the sump route and leaves the leak running.</p>
    <div class="kc-reading">BOW → B · MID → C · STERN → A</div>
    <h3>02 / Restore power and drain</h3>
    <p>Select the backup bus listed for the fault code. A wrong bus trips and costs 8% battery.</p>
    <div class="kc-reading">E1 → 3 · E2 → 1 · E3 → 2</div>
    <p>Switch the load to <strong>Pump</strong> once the leak is sealed and the bus is live. Pump and trim share one supply. Switch off the pump as soon as floodwater is at or below 25% to save battery.</p>
    <h3>03 / Surface</h3>
    <p>With the leak sealed, floodwater at or below 25%, and a working bus, switch the load to <strong>Trim</strong>, then <strong>Blow ballast</strong>. There is one air charge. Trim displays the estimated battery cost and reserve. If the battery runs out during the four-second ascent, the sub stalls below the surface.</p>
    <h3>Dive profiles</h3>
    <p><strong>Hull Breach</strong> is the standard drill. <strong>Below the Shelf</strong> has two depth gauges. E1 and E3 freeze gauge A. E2 freezes gauge B. The live gauge changes while the sub sinks. Set Trim Depth Reference to the live gauge before blowing ballast. The wrong reference spends the air charge and ends the dive.</p>
    <p><strong>Low Reserve</strong> starts with heavy flooding and either a weak battery or very little depth margin. Main pump drains 3.5 floodwater points per second and draws 2.5 battery points per second. Aux pump drains 1.05 and draws 0.48. Both need a sealed leak and a working bus.</p>
    <p>Below about 600 m of depth, compare the time needed to reach 25% with the remaining depth margin. If depth is already over 600 m, use Main and act quickly. If battery is around 37%, use Aux to preserve the roughly 18–20% needed for ascent. Stop pumping at 25%.</p>
    <p>The boat is lost at 650 m, 100% floodwater, or 0% battery.</p>
  </div>;
}
